{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-76514",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence (in function notation) is\n\n$$\nf(n)=f(1)+(n-1)d.\n$$\n\nWe are given the first term $f(1)=4.$ Also, the common difference of the sequence can be found as\n\n$$\nd=-2-4=-6.\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =f(1)+(n-1)d \\\\  & =4+(n-1)(-6) \\\\  & =4-6n+6 \\\\  & =10-6n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is $f(n)=10-6n.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
